    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d62">

                <pb facs="0163" n="68" sameAs="frus:frus1936v04/pg68"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/7812</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 43</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-02-29">February 29, 1936</date>.<lb/>[Received March
                        23.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to report that Mr. Hachiro
                    Arita, newly appointed Ambassador to China, arrived in Shanghai aboard the M. V.
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Asama Maru</hi> on February 26, 1936, accompanied by
                    his wife and Major General Rensuki Isogai, Japanese Military Attaché. His coming
                    was viewed with mixed feelings locally; some fear that he may sympathize with
                    the army and favor a stronger attitude toward China, while others hope that his
                    strength and prestige will enable him to effect a genuine <hi
                        rendition="#italic">rapprochement</hi> without the interference from the
                    military which has been so familiar of late.</p>
                <p>In an interview with representatives of the press he stated that he considers it
                    essential to establish Sino-Japanese relations on a firm footing for the sake of
                    peace and prosperity in East Asia, that he has instructions looking to the
                    general readjustment of relations between the two countries, and that he intends
                    to deal with problems in private talks rather than at a formal conference. He
                    announced it as his intention to reside in Nanking. It is understood that the
                    Counselor of Embassy, Mr. K. Wakasugi, newly transferred from Peiping, will
                    continue to live in Shanghai.</p>
                <p>[Here follows summary of press views.]</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">C. E. Gauss</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
